---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prompt from '../../components/Prompt.astro';
import { plan, money } from '../../data/business';
const audit = plan('audit');
---
<Base title="The evidence — Wardith" description="Read our original self-audit, test your own business for free, and use our prompts to check any provider’s claims.">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Don’t buy on trust alone</p><h1>Put the work<br /><span>under the lens.</span></h1><p class="page-lede">A published audit. Free ways to test your business. Questions you can ask any provider, including us.</p><nav class="page-jump" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#our-evidence">Our findings</a><a href="#test-your-own">Test your business</a><a href="#check-provider">Check a provider</a></nav></div></section>
  <section class="section" id="our-evidence"><div class="shell evidence-summary"><div class="evidence-numbers"><p class="eyebrow">Our original self-audit</p><strong>228</strong><p>recorded answers</p><div class="evidence-small"><p><strong>10</strong>questions</p><p><strong>4</strong>assistants</p><p><strong>Aug ’26</strong>research date</p></div></div><div class="evidence-copy"><h2>The finding that<br />changed our name.</h2><p>In the 168 answers where our old name wasn’t supplied, Noven was never mentioned. In all thirty name-based checks, the assistants described a pharmaceutical business.</p><p>We changed the name to Wardith and kept the original report public, with two declared redactions. These are historical findings under Noven, not current visibility results for Wardith.</p><a class="text-link" href="/ask-your-ai/self-audit/">Read the full original report <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="disclose" style="margin-top: 2rem;"><summary>Ask an assistant to review our work</summary><Prompt
            caption="Paste this into ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini or Copilot"
            label="Prompt for reviewing our audit report"
            intro={`I'm considering paying a small UK firm ${money(audit.price)} for an audit like the one at this link. Read it and tell me straight: is the method sound, or is it dressed-up guesswork? Is the writing honest with the reader, or is it selling? Is ${money(audit.price)} fair for this much work? What's missing that you'd want if this were your business? Where you can't verify something, say so rather than guessing.`}
            note="Give it the link as well as the question — most assistants will read the page. If yours won't, the report is short enough to paste."
          /></details></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section--tint" id="test-your-own"><div class="shell test-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">A free first look</p><h2>Would it<br />recommend you?</h2><p>Use a logged-out or private session where possible. Ask the question your customer would ask, without mentioning your business name.</p><p>A handful of checks gives you a starting point. It is not a reliable score or proof of lost enquiries.</p></div><div><Prompt
            caption="Ask it five times, in five fresh chats"
            label="Prompt for testing your own business"
            intro="Who would you recommend for [what you do] in [where you are]?"
            note="Fresh chats, not five messages in one — earlier answers can influence what comes next. Write down every business it names."
          /><details class="disclose"><summary>How should I read the answers?</summary><p>If you appear repeatedly, you have some evidence of visibility for that question. If you rarely appear, note the businesses that do. If an answer describes you incorrectly, record exactly what it gets wrong.</p><p>Answers vary by assistant, question, location and session.</p></details><details class="disclose"><summary>What does a paid audit add?</summary><p>Repeated testing across more questions and assistants, website and public-source checks, and a written diagnosis. Developer API tests reduce the influence of personal chat history, but don’t perfectly reproduce another person’s experience.</p></details></div></div></section>
  <section class="section" id="check-provider"><div class="shell test-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Before you commission the work</p><h2>Ask the<br />awkward questions.</h2><p>Use the same checks for any provider. Treat an assistant’s response as leads to investigate, not a final verdict.</p></div><div><details class="disclose"><summary>Open the provider-checking prompt</summary><Prompt
            caption="A due-diligence check for any provider, including this one"
            label="Prompt for checking a provider's credibility"
            intro="I'm considering hiring this business. Act as a sceptical due-diligence check and look for credibility signals and warning signs."
            points={[
              'Any accreditations, certifications or memberships they claim — do those bodies genuinely exist, are they independent, and can membership be verified somewhere other than the company’s own website? Flag anything that looks self-issued or pay-to-list.',
              'Any awards or “best of” rankings — who actually awarded them, and is the page making the award hosted by the business receiving it?',
              'Consistency between their claims and their apparent age. Compare claimed years of experience, client numbers and scale against when the domain was registered, when the company was incorporated, and how long their people have listed the business on LinkedIn. Flag anything that doesn’t add up.',
              'Testimonials and case studies — are they attributed to real, findable people and businesses, or are they anonymous?',
              'Any statistics they quote about their industry — do those figures trace back to a real published source?',
              'Do they guarantee outcomes that are actually controlled by someone else?',
              'Who is behind the business — is there a named person, a real address, and published pricing, or is all of that vague?',
            ]}
            outro="Tell me what checks out, what doesn't, and what I should ask them directly. Where you can't verify something, say so rather than guessing."
            note="Add the provider's name and web address at the top before you send it."
          /></details><p class="fine" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">Little public evidence doesn’t prove a problem. Verify important claims at their original sources.</p></div></div></section>
</Base>
